/** \page sigslot Signals and Delegates

@section sigslot_intro Introduction

Callback mechanisms for event handling have become ubiqitous in todays application frameworks. Older examples are to register a callback function pointer with a sender object or the message maps found in the MFC toolkit, more modern approaches include the .NET delegates and so-called signal-slot techniques.
When signals and slots are used, objects can communicate with each other by connecting a signal of one object to the slot of another object. In most cases connection management features are built-in so that an object closes all it connections automatically when it gets destroyed. After the connection has been established, each time a signal is send, all registered slots are being called. Connecting signals to slots is type-safe i.e. a signal can only be connected to a slot that matches the signal's signature. At the same time it allows a great deal of flexibility (loose coupling), since the caller has no intimite knowledge of the callee. A real example may look like this:

@code
int main()
{
    Pt::Gui::Application app;
    Pt::Gui::Button b;
    Pt::connect( b.clicked, app, &Pt::Gui::Application::exit);

    b.setText(L"Quit");
    b.show();
    return app.run();
}
@endcode

The application will simply quit if the button is clicked. The Pt::Gui:.Application object is the callee and the Pt::Gui::Button is the caller, which has a signal called clicked. In this case Pt::Gui::Application::exit is a regular member function.


@section Creating_Signals Creating Signals

Pt::Signal is a class template and its template parameters determine the signature. A Pt::Signal can be instantiated like any other object. If a Pt:.Signal does not have any arguments the parameter list can be left empty.

@code
Pt::Signal<> sig0;                        // Signal without arguments
Pt::Signal<int> sig1;                     // Signal with one argument
Pt::Signal<const std::string&, int> sig2; // Signal with two arguments
@endcode


@section Establishing_Connections Establishing Connections

A signal can be connected to a slot if the signatures are compatible. How a connection is exactly established depends on the type of signal and slot. In most cases Pt::connect is overloaded and provides the easiest way to form a connection. One important feature of Pt::Signal is that the return value of a slot is ignored and therefore a slot is compatible to a Pt::Signal no matter what type it returns. The following code example shows how a Pt::Signal is connected to a free function and a member function:

@code
class Callee : public Pt::Connectable
{
public:
    void slot()
    { printf("Callee::slot() called\n"); }
};

void slot()
{ printf("slot() called.\n"); }

Pt::Signal<> signal;
Callee calee;

Pt::connect( signal, slot );
Pt::connect( signal, callee, &Callee::slot);
@endcode

The first argument to Pt::connect is the signal object, the next ones depend on the callee. When a Pt::Signal is connected to a free function the second parameter to the Pt::connect call is a function pointer. When a Pt::Signal is connected to a member function of an object, the second argument is the callee and the third one the member function pointer. Signals can only be connected to objects that derive from Pt::Connectable to ensure that all connections are closed when the object goes out of scope and no dangling connections are left.

The connecting functions return Connection objects, which can be used to disconnect signals from slots manually. The following code illustrates this:

@code
void slot()
{ printf("slot() called.\n"); }

Pt::Signal<> signal;
Connection c = Pt::connect( signal, slot );
c.valid() // returns true

c.close();
c.valid() // returns false now
@endcode

A Pt::Connection is reference counted and can not be duplicated as such, but always refers to the same shared connection data. If one peer of a connection is destroyed or Pt::Connection::close is called, the connection becomes invalid. A connection can also become invalid if one of the connection peers rejects being connected. Pt::Signal never rejects connections, however other signal types might.

Besides the overloaded versions of Pt::connect, Pt::Signal::connect can be used to connect to any matching slot type, and thus Pt::Signal::connect takes a reference to a Pt::BasicSlot. Slots are lightweight proxy-objects and one example is Pt::MethodSlot, which describes a member function slot. This way, signals can be connected to any callable entity.


@section sigslot3 Sending Signals

Once a connection has been established, signals can be send to invoke the connected slots. This happens by calling Pt::Signal::send with the appropriate arguments, if any.

@code
void tellAge(int age)
{ std::cout << "I am " << age << " years old\n; }

int main ()
{
  Pt::Signal<int> signal;
  Pt::connect( signal, tellAge );
  signal.send(26);
  return 0;
}
@endcode

When the signal is send, the slot is called with the value passed to Pt:.Signal::send. Nothing will happen if the signal is not connected to any slots. When a Pt::Signal is sent, the slot is called immediatly and directly and does not depend on an event loop. If multiple slots are connected to a Pt::Signal, the slots will be called one after another.

*/
